# Trivy gate baseline — pre-existing OS-package findings in the base images.
#
# ── What this file is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It is not a waiver, and it hides nothing. scripts/scan-image.sh runs Trivy
# twice and applies this file to ONE of the two passes:
#
#   report pass  --ignorefile /dev/null   every FIXABLE finding, at every
#                --ignore-unfixed         severity and package type, including
#                                         everything listed here →
#                                         SARIF → GitHub code scanning.
#                                         (Unfixed findings go to the census in
#                                         the job log — see scan-image.sh. Every
#                                         entry in THIS file is fixable by
#                                         definition, so all of them still
#                                         reach code scanning.)
#
#   gate pass    --ignorefile .trivyignore  fixable HIGH/CRITICAL OS packages,
#                                           minus this list → blocks the merge.
#
# The effect is that the gate blocks *regressions*. A newly disclosed CVE, or
# one a PR introduces via a base-image change, goes red on that PR. Debt that
# arrived with a third-party base image Atlas does not build stays visible in
# code scanning without pinning every unrelated PR red.
#
# ── Which two surfaces this file still governs (post-#5361) ───────────────────
#
# It used to apply to every base-image scan on every PR, which is why it kept
# needing hand-maintained entries for CVEs nobody introduced. Since #5361 the
# base tier gates only on a base REFERENCE the PR introduced or changed, so this
# file is consulted on exactly two surfaces:
#
#   1. a NEW or CHANGED base ref, on the PR that introduces it, and
#   2. the BUILT images, which are the gate of record on every trigger.
#
# An unchanged base ref does not gate at all any more, so nothing needs
# suppressing there. That is what makes the entries below a short list rather
# than a standing one — they are not being kept green by hand.
#
# ⚠️ Read that correctly: the four util-linux entries left in the 2026-08-20 pass
# because nothing on that surface is red any more, NOT because the debt was
# cleared. The bare base still carries them; scripts/base-image-gate.sh just
# stopped treating that as a merge-blocking fact. See "What the 2026-08-20 pass
# changed", below.
#
# ── Why there is a baseline at all ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Because two different things get scanned, and only one of them ships.
#
#   BUILT images (deploy/api, deploy/web, deploy/docs) — the artifacts that
#   reach Railway and customers. Their runner stages run `apt-get upgrade` /
#   `apk upgrade`, so every entry below is already patched out of them.
#   Measured 2026-08-12 with the pinned Trivy 0.72.0: ZERO gate-blocking
#   findings in all three.
#
#   BARE base images (oven/bun:1.3.13, caddy:2.11.4-alpine) — scanned by the
#   base-images tier for pin regressions and for DISCOVERY, which is the only
#   thing that notices a new Dockerfile with a base nobody enumerated. Nothing
#   has upgraded these yet at the moment they are scanned, so they still carry
#   the findings below — reported, and blocking only when the PR introduced or
#   changed the reference (#5361).
#
# So the entries here are not a deferral of unfixed debt. They are the gap
# between "what the base ships with" and "what our images ship with", and the
# runner-stage upgrade is what closes it.
#
# ⚠️ An earlier draft of this change emptied the file entirely, on a
# measurement taken against the BUILT images only. CI failed both base-image
# legs immediately. Recording that because the mistake is easy to repeat: a
# green built-image scan says nothing about the bare-base gate, and the two
# numbers are not interchangeable.
#
# ⚠️ Why baselining is legitimate HERE and would not have been before: until
# 2026-08-12 no Atlas image upgraded its packages, so a bare-base CVE really did
# ship — including into the create-atlas docker templates a customer scaffolds
# from. Every runtime stage in the tree now upgrades (deploy/api, deploy/web,
# deploy/docs, packages/sandbox-sidecar, examples/docker, and all three
# create-atlas/templates/docker Dockerfiles). If a future Dockerfile adds a
# runtime stage WITHOUT an upgrade, these entries silently start lying.
#
# That sentence used to be the only thing standing between this file and being
# wrong. It is now a gate: scripts/check-runtime-stage-upgrades.sh fails CI on a
# runtime stage that does not upgrade, and #5361 is why it had to become one —
# under a ref-diff base gate, a new Dockerfile on an EXISTING base with no
# upgrade is invisible to both scan tiers.
#
# ── Scope note ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only OS packages belong here, because only OS packages are gated. Library
# findings inside the images (npm, Go modules) are reported to code scanning but
# never block.
#
# ⚠️ This paragraph used to end "— they are Dependabot's remit, and it can open
# the fix PR." That is false and was already corrected in scripts/scan-image.sh
# by #4878; the correction just never reached this file. Atlas uses bun, and
# `bun` is a separate Dependabot ecosystem from `npm` with NO security updates,
# so no configuration produces a fix PR here. Remediation for library findings
# is hand-authored root `overrides` plus parent bumps. Read the SCOPE section in
# scripts/scan-image.sh for the real detection -> visibility -> remediation
# handoff, and do not restate it here — one copy, one place to keep true.
#
# ── How to re-measure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# After ANY change to a base-image pin or a runner-stage package install,
# re-scan and delete every entry that no longer appears. Scan BOTH surfaces —
# they answer different questions and neither substitutes for the other:
#
#   the bare bases decide what belongs in THIS file (the base-images gate):
#
#     bash scripts/list-runtime-base-images.sh | while read -r i; do \
#       trivy image --scanners vuln --pkg-types os --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#         --ignore-unfixed --ignorefile /dev/null "$i"; done
#
#   the built images decide whether the runner-stage upgrades still work, which
#   is the premise every entry here rests on. Expect ZERO from each:
#
#     docker build -f deploy/api/Dockerfile -t probe-api . && \
#       trivy image --scanners vuln --pkg-types os --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#         --ignore-unfixed --ignorefile /dev/null probe-api
#
# If the second command starts returning findings, do NOT add them here — the
# upgrade has stopped working and that is the bug.
#
# ⚠️ The second command is a LOCAL, UNCACHED build, and that is load-bearing.
# CI builds these images with `cache-from: type=gha`, and
# `RUN apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y` has a stable command string and a
# stable parent, so it cache-hits forever. Measured 2026-08-20 on #5361's first
# CI run — `#68 [runner 2/6] RUN apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y … CACHED`,
# and the built web image carried four util-linux CVEs a fresh build of the same
# commit did not. So "0 from a local build" and "0 from CI" were two different
# claims, and the entries in this file were masking the gap. image-scan.yml now
# passes `no-cache-filters: runner` so the two agree; if they ever disagree
# again, the cache is lying and no entry here is the right response.
#
# Entries are global rather than per-image on purpose: the two runtime bases
# shared 7 of the original 11, so per-image files would duplicate and drift.
#
# ── What the 2026-08-12 pass changed ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# All measurements from Trivy 0.72.0 — the pinned version — run against the
# actual images, not inferred from advisory text.
#
#   deploy/docs    caddy:2.10-alpine -> 2.11.4-alpine, digest-pinned, plus an
#                  `apk upgrade`. 128 unique CVEs -> 10; 6 CRITICAL -> 0;
#                  65 HIGH -> 6; gate-blocking 11 -> 0.
#
#   deploy/api     oven/bun:1.3.13 UNCHANGED (engines.bun holds it there — the
#   deploy/web     #2811 --isolate TLA regression), plus an `apt-get upgrade`.
#                  107 unique CVEs -> 75; 6 CRITICAL -> 4; 29 HIGH -> 18;
#                  gate-blocking 7 -> 0.
#
# The baseline went 11 -> 10 entries, and the 10 are NOT a subset of the 11 —
# the caddy bump retired 4 Alpine findings and introduced 2 curl ones the older
# base did not have. Do not diff the counts and assume continuity.
#
# ⚠️ The `apk upgrade` / `apt-get upgrade` is the load-bearing half, NOT the
# base-image bump. Two things this measured that reading advisories would not:
#
#   - `apk add --no-cache curl` is a SILENT NO-OP when the base already has
#     curl at a lower version. caddy:2.11.4-alpine ships curl 8.19.0-r0 and the
#     fix for CVE-2026-5773 / CVE-2026-6276 is 8.20.0-r0, which sits in the
#     v3.23/main repo the image is already pointed at. Without the explicit
#     upgrade the bump ADDS two gate-blocking CVEs instead of clearing them.
#
#   - Bumping oven/bun to 1.3.14 produces the IDENTICAL 75/4/0 as staying on
#     1.3.13 with the upgrade. The fixes are in the security repo, not the base
#     image, so there was never a security argument for moving that pin — which
#     matters, because moving it would have broken the isolated test runner.
#
# ── What the 2026-08-20 pass changed (#5361) ─────────────────────────────────
#
# No pin moved and no upgrade changed. What moved was WHERE the gate blocks, so
# the numbers below are a baseline for the next reader, not a delta to act on.
# Both surfaces re-measured with the pinned Trivy 0.72.0, per "How to
# re-measure" above:
#
#   BARE bases, gate policy, no baseline applied:
#     caddy:2.11.4-alpine   5 findings   (HIGH 5, CRITICAL 0) — 3 unique CVEs
#     oven/bun:1.3.13      49 findings   (HIGH 47, CRITICAL 2) — 11 unique CVEs
#     14 unique CVEs across both, which is exactly the 14 entries this file held
#     before this pass. The bare bases did NOT get cleaner.
#
#   BUILT images, same policy, no baseline applied — local, uncached builds:
#     probe-api    0        probe-web    0        probe-docs   0
#
#   CI's cached builds of the same commit did NOT agree, and finding out why is
#   the one thing this pass changed beyond where the gate blocks: see the ⚠️
#   under "How to re-measure" above.
#
# So: 14 findings on a surface that ships nothing, 0 on the surface that ships.
# That asymmetry is the whole argument, and it is why the four util-linux
# entries could leave while the other 10 stay listed — the 10 still govern the
# two surfaces named at the top of this file, the four governed neither.
#
# ── If a NEW entry is ever needed ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **Prefer fixing the image.** In order: pick a base without the finding, or a
# newer tag/digest of the same one; failing that, clear it in the runtime stage
# with `apt-get upgrade` / `apk upgrade`, which is what all eight Dockerfiles in
# this tree already do. An entry here is the third option, not the first.
#
# ⚠️ The rule that used to sit here said an entry is correct "only when the
# distro has published no fixed package … or when the fix is unreachable from
# the pinned repo snapshot". Both conditions are FALSE for every entry that has
# ever been in this file, so read literally it forbade all of them (#5362).
# The first is not merely rare, it is UNREACHABLE: scripts/scan-image.sh runs the
# gate pass with --ignore-unfixed, so anything that reaches the gate is fixable
# by construction. A rule every instance violates is worse than no rule — it
# gets worked around by pattern-matching the nearest existing entry, which is
# what #5360 did.
#
# **The actual test**, which is the one the entries already meet and which is
# stated under "Why there is a baseline at all" above:
#
#   Does a runtime stage's `apt-get upgrade` / `apk upgrade` clear this finding
#   from the BUILT image, so that it exists only in the bare base?
#
# Yes → the entry is legitimate. It is the bare-base/built-image gap, not
# deferred debt. No → do not write the entry; the finding ships, and suppressing
# it would make this file lie about what customers receive.
#
# **Verify it, do not assert it.** Run the two-surface re-measure under "How to
# re-measure" above — one copy, one place to keep true. A candidate entry must
# appear in the bare-base scan AND be absent from all three built-image scans.
# A built-image measurement alone is not enough: an earlier draft emptied this
# file on exactly that and CI failed both base legs immediately.
#
# **The one case this is the documented path rather than a fallback** — the
# escape hatch scripts/base-image-gate.sh names when it blocks a new base. A PR
# introducing a NEW or CHANGED base ref gates absolutely on that ref (#5361). If
# the new base's findings are cleared by its own runtime-stage upgrade, that is
# provable on the built image and is precisely the gap above, so a dated entry
# here is the right answer rather than a workaround. Keep this paragraph and
# that failure message consistent — they are the two halves of one instruction.
#
# Always give an entry an `exp:` date — the expiry is the forcing function; once
# passed, Trivy stops honouring it and the gate goes red until it is fixed or
# consciously re-dated. Do not bulk-extend dates — re-scan first.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# oven/bun:1.3.13 (Debian trixie) — runtime base for deploy/api, deploy/web,
# packages/sandbox-sidecar, examples/docker and the create-atlas docker
# templates. All seven are cleared by the `apt-get upgrade` in every runtime
# stage; they persist only in the bare base, which is what base-images scans.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy  3.5.5-1~deb13u1 -> u2
CVE-2026-28387 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-28388 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-28389 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-28390 exp:2026-11-01
# CRITICAL libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-31789 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy  -> 3.5.6-1~deb13u2
CVE-2026-45447 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libcap2  1:2.75-10+b8 -> 1:2.75-10+deb13u1
CVE-2026-4878 exp:2026-11-01
#
# ⚠️ CVE-2026-53612, -53613, -53614 and -53615 were here and are GONE (#5361).
# util-linux and its eight siblings (bsdutils, libblkid1, libmount1,
# libsmartcols1, libuuid1, liblastlog2-2, mount, login), 2.41-5 ->
# 2.41.5-0+deb13u1. The bare base STILL CARRIES ALL FOUR — measured 2026-08-20,
# see "What the 2026-08-20 pass changed" above. They are not listed because
# nothing gates on them any more: they live on an unchanged base ref, which
# #5361 made report-only.
#
# The reason they are worth a paragraph rather than a silent deletion: that one
# util-linux advisory batch reddened every PR in the repo TWICE in eight days
# (53615 on 2026-08-12, then 53612/53613/53614 on 2026-08-20), with an identical
# fixed version both times. A gate going red on the same package twice, for debt
# no PR introduced, is what said the bare base was the wrong thing to hold the
# debt. Re-adding them by hand on the next disclosure is the loop #5361 closed —
# if a util-linux entry ever looks necessary again, check WHY the gate is red
# before writing one.

